# New teaching cards for reading 1. Key: (step, tab index) -> list of (label, html)
N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of learning to drive: at first the instructor decides everything; later you set the route, watch the road and correct yourself. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"srl\">Self-regulated learning</b> is the learning version: learning that comes from your <b>own</b> thoughts and actions, systematically aimed at your own goals (Zimmerman, 2000). The chapter's examples mix thinking, motivation and people: attending to instruction, linking new material to what you know, believing you can learn, and building good working relations and places."),
("Three shared features","All theories agree on three things (Zimmerman, 2001). <b>Active:</b> the learner is involved in her behaviour, her <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metacog\">metacognition</b> (steering her own thinking) and her motivation. <b>Cyclical:</b> it runs in loops: set a goal, monitor progress, respond, for example by working harder or changing strategy. <b>Why:</b> the theories also ask why people choose to regulate, so managing motivation and emotions belongs to it. <i>In plain words:</i> you drive, you check the map, and you keep yourself going."),
("The chapter's plan","The chapter has four parts: <b>five theories</b> (operant, information processing, developmental, social constructivist, social cognitive), <b>research</b> on which processes students use, <b>two programmes</b> that teach self-regulation, and <b>open questions</b>. The topic grew out of research on behavioural self-control (Karoly &amp; Kanfer, 1982). Ideas from Theme 2 (self-efficacy, attributions, goals, mindsets) return here as tools the learner uses on herself."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("The idea","Five photographers can shoot the same student from five angles. Each photo is true, but each shows something else. The chapter explains self-regulation through five theories: <b>operant</b>, <b>information processing</b>, <b>developmental</b>, <b>social constructivist</b> and <b>social cognitive</b>. Operant theory comes first, because much early research came from it and because the other four, which all stress the mind, were built on its context."),
("What each theory stresses","Table 3.1 in one line each. <b>Operant:</b> behaviours you can arrange (self-monitoring, self-instruction, self-reinforcement). <b>Information processing:</b> what the mind does (attention, storage, metacognition, learning strategies). <b>Developmental:</b> what grows with age, including private speech. <b>Social constructivist:</b> the theories learners build about themselves, tasks and strategies, with cultural tools and help in the zone of proximal development. <b>Social cognitive:</b> person, behaviour and environment influencing each other, in a cycle of before, during and after."),
("How they overlap","The five are not separate boxes. Private speech appears in developmental theory (Luria) and in Vygotsky's social constructivist theory. Metacognition appears in information processing and returns in developmental theory. Goals appear everywhere. <i>In plain words:</i> learn what each theory puts in the <b>foreground</b>, because an exam question about a situation tests exactly that."),
("So what","Each lens suggests a different kind of help. Operant: arrange cues, records and rewards. Information processing: teach strategies and comprehension monitoring. Developmental: fit demands to what the child can control at her age. Social constructivist: help in the zone where she can succeed with support, and watch the theories she builds about herself. Social cognitive: models, goals, feedback and the before-during-after cycle. The next steps take the theories one by one."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of training a puppy: cues before, a treat after, and the behaviour you reward comes back more often. Operant theory (Skinner, 1953) applies that logic to yourself. Behaviour happens in the presence of cues (<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"discstim\">discriminative stimuli</b>) and depends on its consequences. Self-regulation then means choosing among actions, usually by <b>giving up an immediate reward for a later, usually greater one</b> (Mace, Belfiore &amp; Shea, 1989). The chapter's Brad studies 6 to 9 p.m. in his room, phone away, and earns a point per good night; 10 points buy a night off."),
("Self-monitoring","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfmon\">Self-monitoring</b> is deliberately watching one aspect of your own behaviour, usually with a record: you cannot regulate what you do not notice. Methods range from frequency counts and duration measures to ratings and lasting records, such as worksheets completed. Two criteria (Bandura, 1986): <b>regularity</b> (record continually, not now and then) and <b>proximity</b> (record right away, not at the end of the day). <i>In plain words:</i> a step counter works because it counts every day, straight away. Self-monitoring alone can already improve behaviour (Belfiore &amp; Hornyak, 1998)."),
("Self-instruction","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfinst\">Self-instruction</b> means setting up cues that trigger the right action: a note to yourself before bed, or statements you say while working. <b>Schunk and Rice (1987):</b> weak readers said a six-step strategy aloud before reading each passage (\"What do I have to do?\" … \"Reread if I don't know the answer\"). Strategy instruction like this improved comprehension and achievement beliefs. <i>In plain words:</i> talking yourself through the steps keeps you on task."),
("Self-reinforcement","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfrein\">Self-reinforcement</b> is rewarding yourself after a response, so the response becomes more likely. But it is <b>unclear whether rewarding yourself works better than being rewarded by a teacher</b>: in class, students may stay on task because of the teacher's control, so the effect is hard to isolate (Brigham, 1982). The reward may matter more than who gives it. Explicit reinforcement may matter more while skills are still being learned."),
("The critique","Behavioural methods help in the short term, for example more time on task. But by looking only at behaviour, operant theory <b>ignores the cognitive and motivational side</b>: beliefs and emotions. Long-term goals, such as finishing a degree, need a richer view. <i>In plain words:</i> the puppy logic explains what you do, not what you think while you do it."),
("So what","Operant theory gives teachers practical tools: cues, honest records, self-talk and self-rewards. Self-monitoring returns later as self-observation in social cognitive theory. What the theory cannot explain is what goes on in the student's head, and that is where the next theory starts."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a thermostat: it compares the room with the setting and heats until they match. Information processing theory sees self-regulation as roughly <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metacog\">metacognition</b>: monitoring, directing and regulating your own thinking toward a goal. It uses three kinds of knowledge: about the <b>task</b>, about <b>yourself</b> and about <b>strategies</b>, plus the know-how to check your learning and decide when to change approach or whether you are ready for a test."),
("The TOTE loop","The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"tote\">TOTE</b> model (Miller, Galanter &amp; Pribram, 1960): <b>Test</b> (compare now with the standard), <b>Operate</b> (change something if they differ), <b>Test</b> again, <b>Exit</b> when they match. The chapter's Jenny reads history and stops to summarize. Her summary falls short of her standard (\"accurate, all topics\"), so she finds the problem, the second paragraph, and rereads it. Then she reads on."),
("Winne and Hadwin's model","Winne and Hadwin's model has four phases: <b>(1) define the task</b>, <b>(2) set goals and plans</b>, <b>(3) use study tactics</b>, and optional <b>(4) adapt</b>, which is skipped when the plan works. To define the task, learners use <b>task conditions</b> (from outside, such as the teacher's instructions) and <b>cognitive conditions</b> (from memory, such as earlier results and how competent they feel). <i>In plain words:</i> work out what is asked, plan, do it, and change course only if it is not working."),
("SMART and COPES","Inside each phase, the mind does five things: <b>SMART</b>, which stands for searching, monitoring, assembling, rehearsing and translating. Each task has five slots: <b>COPES</b>, which stands for conditions, operations, products, evaluations and standards. Products are checked against standards, and the verdict becomes a new condition for the next round. Why it matters for education: the model was built with real study tasks and, unlike older processing models, includes <b>motivation</b>."),
("So what","Information processing puts a small inspector inside the learner: compare, find the gap, fix it, compare again. The inspector needs tools, and those tools are learning strategies (second tab)."),
]
N[(4,1)]=[
("The idea","<b>Learning strategies</b> are mental plans for doing a task well (Weinstein &amp; Mayer, 1986): selecting, organizing, rehearsing and linking information, but also handling test anxiety or building confidence. They are part of self-regulation because they give learners control over their own processing. Think of them as a toolbox with four drawers."),
("Rehearsal","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"rehearse\">Rehearsal</b> means repeating, underlining or summarizing. Repetition works for rote tasks, such as capitals or song lyrics, but it links nothing to what you know, so it is hard to retrieve later. Underlining and summarizing help only when you are selective: mark or keep only the main ideas. <i>In plain words:</i> highlighting the whole page is the same as highlighting nothing."),
("Elaboration and organization","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"elab\">Elaboration</b> adds meaning: mental images, mnemonics (HOMES for the five Great Lakes), asking yourself questions, and notes in your own words. Copying word for word is rehearsal, not elaboration. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"organ\">Organization</b> builds structure: outlining, and mapping the main ideas and how they connect. Mapping improves retention (Nesbit &amp; Adesope, 2006)."),
("Comprehension monitoring","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"compmon\">Comprehension monitoring</b> checks whether it worked. Four moves: <b>self-questioning</b> (who, what, why, how), <b>rereading</b> when you cannot answer, <b>checking consistency</b> (do the parts fit?) and <b>paraphrasing</b> (if you can say it in your own words, no need to reread). <b>Greene and Azevedo (2009):</b> in a hypermedia science environment, monitoring, especially self-questioning, clearly improved students' understanding."),
("So what","Rehearsal holds information, elaboration adds meaning, organization builds structure, and monitoring checks the result. Monitoring is what makes the toolbox self-regulated: the learner decides when to reach for another tool. Reading 2 is entirely about that drawer."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","A child cannot ride a bike before her legs are long enough, however well you explain it. Developmental theorists see self-regulation as <b>progressive cognitive changes</b> that give learners more control over thoughts, feelings and actions (Paris, Byrnes &amp; Paris, 2001): starting and stopping actions, delaying, acting in socially approved ways."),
("Kopp's phases","Kopp (1982) traces control from birth. 0 to 3 months: arousal. 3 to 9 months: behaviour adjusts to events. 9 to 12 months: first <b>compliance</b> with caregivers. Second year: <b>impulse control</b>, and the critical shift from outside to inside control as children start to internalize rules. Third year: <b>self-control</b>, helped by language. Fourth year: <b>self-regulation</b>, adopting rules and adapting behaviour to the situation."),
("What grows with age","Attention, memory, processing speed and metacognition all improve. A 5-year-old repeats about four digits, a 12-year-old six or seven. When decoding becomes <b>automatic</b>, attention is freed for comprehension. Metacognitive understanding expands greatly between ages 5 and 10. <i>For teachers:</i> forewarn about attention demands, use outlines and study guides as advance organizers, and keep students focused with prompts and feedback."),
("Private speech","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"privsp\">Private speech</b> is talking to steer yourself, not to communicate. Luria (1961) described three stages: first others' words steer the child (about 1½ to 2½ years); then her own words can start an action but not stop it (3 to 4); then her private speech can start, direct and stop it (4½ to 5½). <i>In plain words:</i> the voice that guides you moves from your parents' mouth to your own head."),
("Three deficiencies","Why do children not use helpful self-talk? A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"prodef\">production deficiency</b> means they do not produce it. A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"meddef\">mediational deficiency</b> means they say it, but it does not steer what they do. A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"contdef\">continued-use deficiency</b> means they stop once nobody asks them to. The remedies: more instruction with spaced review, practice on several tasks, and <b>strategy value information</b> (telling them the strategy helps, and linking it to their progress)."),
("So what","Development sets the stage: how much a child can control, attend to and hold in mind, and whether her own words can steer her. So teachers fit demands to the child's level, and teach not only a strategy but also why it works, on several tasks."),
]
N[(5,1)]=[
("The idea","Every learner is a small scientist who builds theories about herself: \"I am bad at maths\", \"this task needs effort\", \"flashcards work for me\". Social constructivists assume people are naturally motivated to learn, impose meaning on what they meet, refine their understanding throughout life, and are limited by their readiness. Self-regulation here means <b>forming theories</b> about your abilities, about tasks and about how to use effort and strategies."),
("Building theories","These theories change with development: young children do not separate ability from effort, and a distinct idea of ability appears later (Nicholls, 1978). Learners also build their own versions of strategies. Theories can be wrong: a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"buggy\">buggy algorithm</b>, such as always subtracting the smaller digit from the larger, gives answers and even a <b>false sense of self-efficacy</b> (Brown &amp; Burton, 1978)."),
("Mindsets and identity","Dweck's <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"mindset\">mindsets</b> are theories about your own capacity to learn: <b>fixed</b> (limited, effort cannot change it) or <b>growth</b> (you can keep improving). Growth-mindset learners engage more productively and persevere. In the end, learners build an <b>identity as a student</b>, shaped by others, including stereotypes; identity and self-regulation cannot be separated (Paris et al., 2001)."),
("Vygotsky","Vygotsky gave language the central role: children internalize the meaning of others' words and use them to steer themselves. He expected thinking aloud to rise until about 6 or 7 and then go inward by 8 to 10. Research is mixed: from about 4 or 5 to 8, children talk to themselves less, but a growing <b>share</b> of it is self-regulating (Fuson, 1979). His <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"zpd\">zone of proximal development</b> holds tasks a learner can do with help but not yet alone; support (scaffolding) fades as she internalizes the skill."),
("So what","For teachers: give help in the zone and fade it, and listen to the theories students hold about themselves, tasks and strategies, because a wrong theory steers behaviour as surely as a right one. Next: the theory that runs through this whole theme."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("The idea","In social cognitive theory, self-regulation lives where learners have <b>choices</b>. Zimmerman (1998) names six areas: <b>motives, methods, time, outcomes, physical environment and social environment</b>. If a teacher fixes all six, students can still learn, but the control lies outside them. <i>In plain words:</i> no choice, no self-regulation."),
("Three-way influence","Bandura's (1986) <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"recip\">reciprocal interactions</b>: personal factors, behaviour and environment influence each other (Figure 3.1). Example: self-efficacy steers how hard a student works; progress on her paper then raises her self-efficacy (behaviour → person); a teacher who expects little of a student with a learning disability can lower that student's self-efficacy (environment → person), while \"I know you can do this\" can raise it."),
("Self-judgment and self-reaction","Three classic processes (Bandura, 1986): <b>self-observation</b> (much like self-monitoring), <b>self-judgment</b> (comparing your performance with your goal) and <b>self-reaction</b> (how you respond). Seeing progress raises self-efficacy and motivation. Even a poor result need not lower motivation, if you believe you can improve. Higher goals bring more effort, as long as you believe you can reach them (Locke &amp; Latham, 2002)."),
("So what","The social cognitive learner is never a closed system: beliefs steer behaviour, behaviour changes the setting, and the setting talks back to beliefs. The next tab orders these processes in time."),
]
N[(6,1)]=[
("The idea","Think of a sports match: preparation, the match itself, the review afterwards, which shapes the next preparation. Zimmerman's (2000) <b>three-phase cycle</b> adds \"before\" to the classic \"during and after\": <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"forethought\">forethought</b>, <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"perfctrl\">performance control</b> and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfrefl\">self-reflection</b>. Other models name similar phases (Pintrich, 2000; Lord et al., 2010)."),
("What happens in each phase","<b>Forethought:</b> set goals, plan strategies, believe you can (self-efficacy). <b>Performance control:</b> use the strategies, monitor and record how it goes. <b>Self-reflection:</b> judge your progress, explain the result, keep or change the strategy, set new goals. Then the cycle restarts: one round's reflection becomes the next round's forethought."),
("The evidence","<b>DiBenedetto and Zimmerman (2010):</b> high-achieving science students used more self-regulation in all three phases than average and low achievers. <b>Cleary, Zimmerman and Keating (2006):</b> novice basketball players learned one, two or three phases before practising free throws. <b>Found:</b> the three-phase group made the most adaptive attributions and strategy corrections; the two- and three-phase groups shot most accurately. <i>In plain words:</i> teaching goal setting alone is not enough."),
("So what","The cycle turns a study session into a loop that improves itself. For teachers, the free-throw study says: teach students to monitor during the task and reflect afterwards, not only to set goals. Reading 3 works this cycle out in depth."),
]
N[(6,2)]=[
("The idea","Nobody learns to cook alone from scratch: you watch, copy, cook alone with the recipe, then improvise. Schunk and Zimmerman (1997) proposed that self-regulation develops first from <b>social sources</b> and then from <b>self-sources</b>, in four levels."),
("The four levels","<b>Observation:</b> watching models shows the main features of a strategy. <b>Emulation:</b> practising the general pattern with guidance and feedback, imitating the style rather than copying. <b>Self-control:</b> using the strategy alone, but still following the model's way. <b>Self-regulation:</b> adapting the strategy to new situations, driven by goals and self-efficacy. Levels one and two are social; three and four are internal."),
("Social help never disappears","Even advanced learners keep using teachers, coaches and peers to refine their skills (Zimmerman, 2000). Self-regulation does not mean social independence; it means taking more and more control of your own learning. Learning alone is possible, but it misses what the social environment offers."),
("So what","The levels give a teaching plan: show it, let students try it with feedback, let them do it alone, then let them adapt it. Steps 8 and 9 show experiments and a programme built on this."),
]
N[(7,0)]=[
("The idea","Research first asked which processes students actually use. <b>Zimmerman and Martinez-Pons (1986)</b> interviewed students about eight situations, such as writing a paper or preparing for a test. Their answers fell into <b>fourteen categories</b> (Table 3.2), from goal setting and planning, self-evaluating and keeping records, to environmental structuring, self-consequating, reviewing, and seeking help from peers, teachers or adults."),
("Who uses which","<b>Zimmerman and Martinez-Pons (1990)</b>, grades 5, 8 and 11. <b>Found:</b> older students used their notes more than textbooks, asked teachers more than parents, and kept more records, organized more and planned more. Girls reported more record keeping, environmental structuring and goal setting than boys. Gifted students organized more, rewarded themselves more and sought more help from peers. <i>In plain words:</i> self-regulation grows with age and differs between groups."),
("Measuring it: the MSLQ","<b>Pintrich and De Groot (1990)</b> used the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"mslq\">MSLQ</b>, a questionnaire with two parts: <b>motivational beliefs</b> (self-efficacy, intrinsic value, test anxiety) and <b>learning strategies</b> (cognitive strategy use, self-regulation). An item might ask whether you put important ideas into your own words. Keeping positive beliefs about learning is itself a self-regulation strategy (Zimmerman, 2011)."),
("So what","Self-regulation is a repertoire, not one skill, and students differ in which parts they use. Three of the fourteen categories are about asking others for help. Next: goals, studied in a series of experiments."),
]
N[(7,1)]=[
("The idea","Goal setting is the core of forethought. But which goals work best? Researchers varied the <b>properties</b> of goals (Locke &amp; Latham, 2002): how near they are, how hard, and whether they aim at learning a process or at a result."),
("Near goals and hard goals","<b>Bandura and Schunk (1981):</b> children practising subtraction aimed at one set per session (near goal), all sets by the end (distant goal), or simply working hard (general goal). <b>Found:</b> near goals gave the best practice, self-efficacy and achievement; the distant goal was no better than the general one. <b>Schunk (1983a):</b> difficult but reachable goals raised motivation and achievement; being told \"you can do this\" raised self-efficacy. <i>In plain words:</i> close and challenging beats far away and vague."),
("Process goals and feedback","<b>Schunk and Swartz (1993):</b> children learning to write paragraphs had a process goal (learn the strategy), a product goal (write paragraphs) or a general goal (do your best); half the process-goal children also got progress feedback. <b>Found:</b> process goal plus feedback was most effective on self-efficacy, writing, self-evaluated progress and strategy use; gains held after 6 weeks and transferred to new paragraph types. <i>In plain words:</i> aim at learning the method and see your progress."),
("Shifting goals","<b>Zimmerman and Kitsantas (1996, 1997):</b> girls learning darts focused on the throwing steps (process goal) or on their score (product goal). Process goals and self-recording raised self-efficacy and performance. A <b>shifting goal</b>, first the steps and then the score once the steps were automatic, worked best of all. <i>In plain words:</i> learn the technique first, then aim for results."),
("So what","Four lessons (our summary): make goals near; make them hard but reachable; start with learning the process, with progress feedback; switch to outcome goals once the process is automatic. That switch is the move from self-control to self-regulation."),
]
N[(7,2)]=[
("The idea","Working toward a goal, learners need to see they are getting closer, but progress is often hard to see. <b>Progress feedback</b> supports self-efficacy and motivation, and skilled learners get better at judging their own progress."),
("Self-evaluation","<b>Schunk (1996):</b> students learning fractions had a learning goal (learn how) or a performance goal (just solve); half in each group evaluated their own skills after each session. <b>Found:</b> every group except \"performance goal, no self-evaluation\" improved in self-efficacy, skill and motivation. With infrequent self-evaluation, only learning goals helped. Schunk and Ertmer (1999) found the same with college students learning computer skills. <i>In plain words:</i> evaluate often, and if you rarely do, at least aim at learning."),
("Self-monitoring","<b>Sagotsky, Patterson and Lepper (1978):</b> pupils who monitored their maths work increased time on task and achievement; daily goal setting had little effect, perhaps because they had not learned to set realistic goals. <b>Schunk (1983b):</b> pupils who recorded their own progress and pupils whose progress an adult recorded improved equally, and more than pupils without recording, with the same amount of work. <i>In plain words:</i> what helps is making progress visible, not who writes it down."),
("So what","Make progress visible: frequent self-evaluation lifts results, and recording helps whoever does it. Our tip for teachers: record progress for young learners first, then hand the recording over. Next: learning from others."),
]
N[(8,0)]=[
("The idea","Watching someone succeed teaches you the steps and also that you can do it. Models raise <b>self-efficacy</b> and pass on <b>standards</b>, especially when the observer feels similar to the model (Schunk &amp; Pajares, 2009)."),
("Models and self-efficacy","<b>Zimmerman and Ringle (1981):</b> children who watched a pessimistic model struggle for a long time with a puzzle lowered their own self-efficacy. <b>Schunk (1981):</b> adult modeling and written instruction in division both raised self-efficacy, persistence and achievement, but modeling raised achievement more and made self-efficacy more accurate. <i>In plain words:</i> what a model shows and says rubs off on you, good or bad."),
("Coping versus mastery models","A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"coping\">coping model</b> struggles, makes mistakes and overcomes them; a mastery model performs flawlessly. <b>Schunk and Hanson (1985):</b> peer models beat an adult model and no model. <b>Schunk, Hanson and Cox (1987):</b> with fractions, a task the children had failed before, <b>peer coping models</b> worked better than peer mastery models, and several peer models did as well as one coping model. <i>In plain words:</i> beginners learn more from someone who visibly struggles and recovers."),
("Watching yourself, and the four levels","<b>Schunk and Hanson (1989):</b> children who watched videos of themselves solving problems gained self-efficacy, motivation and strategy use (<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfmodel\">self-modeling</b>). <b>Zimmerman and Kitsantas (2002):</b> college students learning to revise sentences learned most from a coping model, then a mastery model, then no model; feedback during practice helped in every condition. The dart study tested the step from self-control to self-regulation."),
("So what","The best model for a beginner is often not the most brilliant one, but a peer who struggles, recovers and succeeds, or several peers, so every student finds someone like herself. Next tab: the help a learner gives herself by talking."),
]
N[(8,1)]=[
("The idea","Saying your strategy aloud can steer you while you work. This is the research side of private speech (step 5) and self-instruction (step 3)."),
("Two experiments","<b>Schunk (1982):</b> low-achieving children learning long division said the model's strategy words, made up their own, did both, or stayed silent. <b>Found:</b> own words gave the most self-directed practice and the highest achievement; both together gave the highest self-efficacy. <b>Schunk and Cox (1986):</b> children with learning disabilities said the subtraction steps aloud throughout, stopped halfway, or never did. <b>Found:</b> talking aloud throughout gave the highest self-efficacy and achievement. The authors' tentative explanation: those who stopped may have dropped the words that steered them."),
("So what","Self-talk works best in the learner's own words, and students may need to <b>fade</b> it into silent self-talk rather than stop suddenly. The whisper stage in the picture and the link to Vygotsky's curve are ours."),
]
N[(9,0)]=[
("The idea","Self-regulation does not simply grow with age; it has to be taught. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"srsd\">Self-Regulated Strategy Development</b> (SRSD; Harris, Graham &amp; Mason, 2006) teaches a task strategy together with self-regulation (goals, self-monitoring, managing effort), through teacher modeling, practice with peers and independent practice, with support that fades. It has helped writing, reading and mathematics."),
("The two mnemonics","<b>Reid and Lienemann (2006)</b> used SRSD for story writing with children with ADHD. Stories had to contain <b>seven parts</b>: <b>WWW, What = 2, How = 2</b> (who, where, when; what they want, what happens next; how it ends, how they feel). Planning followed <b>POW</b>: Pick my ideas, Organize my notes, Write and say more."),
("The five stages","(1) <b>Develop background knowledge:</b> learn the mnemonics and find the parts in stories. (2) <b>Discuss it:</b> check your own stories and chart your parts. (3) <b>Model it:</b> the teacher writes a story aloud with self-statements (\"What is my goal?\", \"I like that part!\"), and students make their own. (4) <b>Support it:</b> write together with organizers. (5) <b>Independent performance:</b> write alone and graph your progress."),
("The results","Stories grew significantly in parts and words, and the gains held 3 and 6 weeks later. SRSD in general improves strategy use, performance and motivation, and supports <b>maintenance and transfer</b>. Nobody measured Zimmerman's four levels, but the stages build in observation, emulation and self-control, and the results hint that many students may have reached self-regulation."),
("So what","SRSD is the five theories turned into a lesson plan: self-monitoring and self-reward (operant), strategies (information processing), fading support (Vygotsky), models and social-to-self levels (social cognitive)."),
]
N[(9,1)]=[
("The students","<b>Zimmerman, Moylan, Hudesman, White and Flugman (2011)</b> worked with at-risk undergraduates in mathematics at an urban technological college, where only 21% finished an associate degree within 6 years. Many were <b>over-confident</b>: their self-efficacy was poorly <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"calib\">calibrated</b>, which blocks learning from feedback."),
("The intervention","A semester-long <b>randomized controlled</b> study, built on the reflection phase. Instructors modeled how to correct errors. After every quiz, students filled in a <b>correction form</b>: compare your confidence with your result, explain which strategy failed, choose a better one, and rate your confidence for a similar problem. Completing the form earned extra points. The message: errors are chances to learn, not end points."),
("The results","The trained group <b>outperformed</b> the control group on maths exams, <b>over-estimated itself less</b> before and after solving problems, and passed a national gateway maths exam <b>25 percentage points</b> more often. Within the trained classes, students who reflected more scored higher. Because students were randomly assigned, the effects can be read as causal, and they were large or near large. <i>In plain words:</i> learning from each quiz turned over-confidence into better results."),
("So what","This programme strengthens the self-reflection phase: holding confidence against results and planning a better strategy turns feedback into the forethought of the next round."),
]
N[(10,0)]=[
("Development and the home","Two literatures need to meet: development and self-regulation. Key question: is it better to <b>teach</b> children strategies, or help them <b>construct</b> their own? The authors propose comparing the two, with peer teachers in both conditions. Parents also differ greatly in how much they teach self-regulation, so long-term studies of the home are needed."),
("The curriculum","Study skills taught separately may not transfer. Taught <b>inside a subject</b>, self-regulation improved motivation, learning and achievement in reading, writing, science and mathematics (Zimmerman &amp; Schunk, 2011). It also shows students the <b>value</b> of strategies, and students who see no value will not use them. The authors' example: one teacher teaches term-paper strategies and the maths, science and history teachers show how to adapt them."),
("Culture","Assuming self-regulation works the same in every culture is <b>unwarranted</b> (McInerney, 2011). Students from cultures that stress effort and structure, such as China and Japan, may thrive in structured settings but show less self-regulated behaviour in open ones. That does not mean they do not self-regulate: regulating effort and persistence is part of it too."),
("Technology","Computer and distance learning lack the outside controls of a classroom, so self-regulation becomes critical there. Research should find which strategies help and how technology can support planning, monitoring and reflection (Azevedo). Distance students may benefit from learning how to interact with peers (Bernard et al., 2009)."),
("The conclusion","Self-regulation is now a central topic in learning research. Each of the theories has important implications for research and practice, and the authors expect more intervention studies to show how best to teach it. <i>In plain words:</i> we know self-regulation matters and can be taught; the next questions are how, for whom and where."),
]

