N[(2,0)][1]=("What each theory stresses","Table 3.1 in one line each. <b>Operant:</b> behaviours you can arrange (self-monitoring, self-instruction, self-reinforcement). <b>Information processing:</b> what the mind does (attention, coding, storage, retrieval, <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metacog\">metacognition</b>) and learning strategies. <b>Developmental:</b> what grows with age, including <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"privsp\">private speech</b>. <b>Social constructivist:</b> theories learners build about themselves, tasks and strategies; cultural tools such as languages and symbols; the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"zpd\">zone of proximal development</b>. <b>Social cognitive:</b> person, behaviour and environment influencing each other, in a cycle of forethought, performance control and self-reflection.")
N[(2,0)][2]=("How they overlap","The five are not separate boxes. Private speech appears in developmental theory (Luria) and in Vygotsky's social constructivist theory. Metacognition appears in information processing and returns in developmental theory. <i>Our tip:</i> learn what each theory puts in the <b>foreground</b>, because an exam question about a situation tests exactly that.")
N[(2,0)][3]=("So what","Each lens suggests a different kind of help (our summary). Operant: arrange cues, records and rewards. Information processing: teach strategies and comprehension monitoring. Developmental: fit demands to what the child can control. Social constructivist: help in the zone and watch the theories she builds about herself. Social cognitive: models, goals, feedback and the cycle. The next steps take the theories one by one.")
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of training a puppy: cues before, a treat after. Operant theory (Skinner, 1953) applies that logic to yourself. Behaviour happens in the presence of cues (<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"discstim\">discriminative stimuli</b>) and depends on its consequences: praise that makes a pupil answer more often is a <b>reinforcer</b>; criticism that makes her answer less is a <b>punishment</b>. Self-regulation means choosing among actions, usually by <b>giving up an immediate reward for a later, usually greater one</b> (Mace, Belfiore &amp; Shea, 1989)."),
("Brad's plan","The chapter's Brad studies too little. With his counsellor he sets a fixed time and place (6 to 9 p.m. in his room, two short breaks), puts his phone away, and earns a point per successful night; 10 points buy a night off. The routine in four moves (Mace et al., 2001): choose the behaviour, set up cues, compare performance with a standard, reinforce. Three key processes follow: self-monitoring, self-instruction and self-reinforcement."),
("Self-monitoring","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfmon\">Self-monitoring</b> is deliberately watching one aspect of your own behaviour, usually with a record. Six methods: narrations, frequency counts, duration measures, time-sampling, behaviour ratings, and lasting records such as worksheets completed. Two criteria (Bandura, 1986): <b>regularity</b> (record continually) and <b>proximity</b> (record right away). <i>In plain words:</i> a step counter works because it counts every day, straight away. Self-monitoring alone can already improve behaviour (Belfiore &amp; Hornyak, 1998)."),
]+N[(3,0)][2:]
N[(4,0)][2]=("Winne and Hadwin's model","Winne and Hadwin (1998, 2008) describe four phases: <b>(1) define the task</b>, <b>(2) set goals and plans</b>, <b>(3) use study tactics</b>, and optional <b>(4) adapt</b>, which is skipped when the plan works. To define the task, learners use <b>task conditions</b> (from outside, such as the teacher's instructions) and <b>cognitive conditions</b> (from memory, such as earlier results and how competent they feel). <i>In plain words:</i> work out what is asked, plan, do it, and change course only if it is not working.")
N[(4,0)]=N[(4,0)][:3]+[
("SMART","Inside each phase, the mind does five things, <b>SMART</b>: <b>searching</b> memory for what you know, <b>monitoring</b> (comparing what you found with what you want), <b>assembling</b> (linking new to old), <b>rehearsing</b> (repeating so it is not lost) and <b>translating</b> (changing the format, for example from images to words)."),
("COPES","Every task has five slots, <b>COPES</b>, which are what a student \"copes\" with: <b>conditions</b> (beliefs, motivation, prior knowledge), <b>operations</b> (strategies), <b>products</b> (what comes out), <b>evaluations</b> (does it meet the standard?) and <b>standards</b> (the qualities it should have). The verdict becomes a new condition for the next round. Why it matters: the model was built with real study tasks and includes <b>motivation</b>."),
]+N[(4,0)][4:]
N[(5,0)][1]=("Kopp's phases","Kopp (1982) traces control from birth. 0 to 3 months: <b>neurophysiological modulation</b> (arousal). 3 to 9 months: <b>sensorimotor modulation</b> (behaviour adjusts to events). 9 to 12 months: first <b>compliance</b> with caregivers. Second year: <b>impulse control</b>, and the critical shift from outside to inside control. Third year: <b>self-control</b>, helped by language. Fourth year: <b>self-regulation</b>, adopting rules and adapting behaviour to the situation.")
N[(5,0)][3]=("Private speech","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"privsp\">Private speech</b> is talking to steer yourself, not to communicate. Its roots lie in Pavlov's idea of a second, language-based signal system in humans. Luria (1961) described three stages: first others' words steer the child (about 1½ to 2½); then her own words can start an action but cannot yet reliably stop it (3 to 4); then her private speech can start, direct and stop it (4½ to 5½). <i>In plain words:</i> the guiding voice moves from your parents to your own head.")
N[(5,0)][4]=("Three deficiencies","Why do children not use helpful self-talk? <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"prodef\">Production deficiency:</b> they do not produce it. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"meddef\">Mediational deficiency:</b> they say it, but it does not steer them. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"contdef\">Continued-use deficiency:</b> they stop when no longer asked. Three causes and remedies: weak understanding → repeated instruction with spaced review; strategy tied to the training setting → several tasks and training in modifying it; benefit not seen → <b>strategy value information</b>.")
N[(5,1)]=[
("The idea","Every learner is a small scientist who builds theories about herself: \"I am bad at maths\", \"flashcards work for me\". Social constructivists assume people are naturally motivated to learn and impose meaning on what they meet; their mental representations change with development, using cultural tools such as language; understanding keeps being refined; readiness limits learning; and reflection and reconstruction drive it. Self-regulation means <b>forming theories</b> about your abilities, tasks and strategies."),
N[(5,1)][1],N[(5,1)][2],
("Private speech curve","Vygotsky gave language the central role: children internalize the meaning of others' words and use them to steer themselves. He expected thinking aloud to rise until about 6 or 7 and then go inward by 8 to 10. Research: from about 4 or 5 to 8, children talk to themselves less, but a growing <b>share</b> of it is self-regulating (Fuson, 1979). Many children hardly talk aloud at all, so the pattern is more complex than he expected."),
("Zone of proximal development","The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"zpd\">zone of proximal development</b> holds tasks a learner can do with help but not yet alone. While teachers or peers support her (scaffolding), she works more and more independently as she internalizes the skill, and the zone moves on to harder learning. <i>In plain words:</i> help at the edge of what you can do, then let go."),
N[(5,1)][4],
]
N[(6,0)][1]=("Three-way influence","Bandura's (1986) <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"recip\">reciprocal interactions</b> (Figure 3.1): person, behaviour and environment influence each other. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"seff\">Self-efficacy</b>, your belief that you can learn or perform at a given level, shows it. Person ↔ behaviour: self-efficacy steers effort, and progress raises self-efficacy. Person ↔ environment: low expectations of a pupil with a learning disability can lower her self-efficacy; \"I know you can do this\" can raise it. Behaviour ↔ environment: pupils look where the teacher points; wrong answers make the teacher reteach.")
N[(6,1)][0]=("The idea","Think of a sports match: preparation, the match, the review that shapes the next preparation. Zimmerman's (2000) <b>three-phase cycle</b> adds \"before\" to the classic \"during and after\": <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"forethought\">forethought</b> (before), <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"perfctrl\">performance (volitional) control</b> (during) and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfrefl\">self-reflection</b> (after the task and during pauses). Other models name similar phases (Pintrich, 2000; Lord et al., 2010).")
N[(6,1)][2]=("The evidence","<b>DiBenedetto and Zimmerman (2010):</b> high-achieving science students used more self-regulation in all three phases. <b>Cleary, Zimmerman and Keating (2006):</b> novice basketball players learned one phase (set goals), two (also self-record) or three (also make <b>attributions</b>, the cause you give for a miss, and adjust strategy). <b>Found:</b> three phases gave the most adaptive attributions and corrections; two and three phases shot most accurately. <i>In plain words:</i> goal setting alone is not enough.")
N[(7,0)][0]=("The idea","Research first asked which processes students use. <b>Zimmerman and Martinez-Pons (1986)</b> interviewed students about eight situations, such as writing a paper or preparing a test. Answers fell into <b>fourteen categories</b> (Table 3.2), including self-evaluating; organizing and transforming; goal setting and planning; seeking information; keeping records and monitoring; environmental structuring; self-consequating; rehearsing and memorizing; seeking help from peers, teachers or adults; and reviewing tests, notes or texts.")
N[(7,0)][1]=("Who uses which","<b>Zimmerman and Martinez-Pons (1990)</b>, grades 5, 8 and 11. <b>Found:</b> compared with younger students, older students reviewed notes more and textbooks less, asked teachers more and parents less, and kept more records, organized more and planned more. Girls reported more record keeping, environmental structuring and goal setting than boys. Gifted students organized more, rewarded themselves more and sought more peer help. <i>In plain words:</i> self-regulation grows with age and differs between groups.")
N[(7,2)][1]=("Self-evaluation","<b>Schunk (1996):</b> students learning fractions had a learning goal (learn how) or a performance goal (just solve); half in each group evaluated their own skills after each session. <b>Found:</b> both learning-goal groups and the self-evaluating performance group reached higher self-efficacy, skill and motivation than the performance group without self-evaluation. With infrequent self-evaluation, only learning goals helped. Schunk and Ertmer (1999) found the same with college students. <i>In plain words:</i> evaluate often, and if you rarely do, aim at learning.")
N[(8,0)][1]=("Models and self-efficacy","<b>Zimmerman and Ringle (1981):</b> children who watched a pessimistic model struggle long with a puzzle lowered their own self-efficacy. <b>Brown and Inouye (1978):</b> students told they were more competent than a failing model kept higher self-efficacy and persisted longer. <b>Schunk (1981):</b> modeling and written instruction both raised self-efficacy and achievement, but modeling raised achievement more and made self-efficacy more accurate. <i>In plain words:</i> what a model shows rubs off on you.")
N[(8,0)][2]=("Coping versus mastery models","A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"coping\">coping model</b> struggles and recovers; a mastery model is flawless. <b>Schunk and Hanson (1985)</b>, subtraction: peer coping and peer mastery models worked equally well, both better than an adult model, which beat no model. <b>Schunk, Hanson and Cox (1987)</b>, fractions, a task the children had failed before: <b>peer coping models</b> beat peer mastery models, and several peer models did better than one mastery model. <i>In plain words:</i> when a task is new or hard, a struggling peer helps most.")
N[(8,0)][3]=("Watching yourself, and the four levels","<b>Schunk and Hanson (1989):</b> children who watched videos of themselves solving problems gained self-efficacy, motivation and strategy use (<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfmodel\">self-modeling</b>). <b>Zimmerman and Kitsantas (2002):</b> watching a model was the observation level, practice with or without feedback the emulation level. Coping model beat mastery model beat no model, and feedback helped. The dart study (1997) covered the next step: from self-control (doing the steps) to self-regulation (adapting them to hit a score).")
N[(9,0)][0]=("The idea","Self-regulation does not simply grow with age; it has to be taught. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"srsd\">Self-Regulated Strategy Development</b> (SRSD; Harris, Graham &amp; Mason, 2006) teaches a task strategy together with self-regulation (goals, self-monitoring, managing effort), through teacher modeling, practice with peers and independent practice, with support that fades. It has been applied to writing, reading and mathematics, with positive effects on learning, motivation and strategy use.")
N[(9,0)][4]=("So what","Our summary: SRSD is the five theories turned into a lesson plan: self-monitoring and self-reward (operant), strategies (information processing), fading support (Vygotsky), and models and social-to-self levels (social cognitive).")
N[(9,1)][0]=("The students","<b>Zimmerman, Moylan, Hudesman, White and Flugman (2011)</b> worked with at-risk undergraduates in mathematics at an urban technological college, where only 21% finished an associate degree within 6 years. Many were <b>over-confident</b>: their self-efficacy was poorly <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"calib\">calibrated</b>, which can get in the way of learning from feedback.")
N[(10,0)][0]=("Development and the home","Two literatures need to meet: development and self-regulation. Key question: is it better to <b>teach</b> children strategies, or help them <b>construct</b> their own? The authors propose comparing direct teaching with group construction, using peers as teachers in the direct condition so the type of model is held equal. Parents also differ greatly in how much they teach self-regulation, so long-term studies of the home are needed.")
N[(10,0)][2]=("Culture","The authors call it <b>unwarranted</b> to assume self-regulation works the same in every culture: cultures differ in values and beliefs (McInerney, 2011). Students from cultures that stress effort and structure, such as China and Japan (McInerney, 2008), may thrive in structured settings but show less self-regulated behaviour in open ones. That does not mean they do not self-regulate: regulating effort and persistence is part of it too.")
N[(10,0)][3]=("Technology","Computer and distance learning lack the outside controls of a classroom, so self-regulation becomes critical there. Research should find which strategies help and how technology can support planning, monitoring and reflection (Azevedo). Bernard et al. (2009): more and better interaction between students may help learning; the authors suggest tutorials on interacting with peers before a distance course starts.")
